W: 1. As down the glen one Easter morn
W: To a city fair rode I
W: There armed lines of marching men
W: In squadrons passed me by
W: No pipe did hum
W: No battle drum did sound its loud tattoo
W: But the Angelus Bells o'er the Liffey swells
W: Rang out in the foggy dew
W:
W: 2. Right proudly high in Dublin town
W: Hung they out a flag of war
W: 'Twas better to die 'neath that Irish sky
W: Than at Sulva or Sud-El-Bar
W: And from the plains of Royal Meath
W: Strong men came hurrying through
W: While Brittania's Huns with their long range guns
W: Sailed in through the foggy dew
W:
W: 3. Their bravest fell and the requiem bell
W: Rang mournfully and clear
W: For those who died that Eastertide in the
W: Springing of the year
W: While the world did gaze with deep amaze
W: At those fearless men but few
W: Who bore the fight that freedom's light
W: Might shine through the foggy dew
W:
W: 4. And back through the glen, I rode again
W: And my heart with grief was sore
W: For I parted then with valiant men
W: Whom I never shall see n'more
W: But to and fro in my dreams I go
W: And I kneel and pray for you
W: For slavery fled, O glorious dead
W: When you fell in the foggy dew
%
